    "lawCode": "Amd §415, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1763             REVISED 02/10/2021\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to certificates\nof registration for entities that manufacture or assemble zero emissions\nvehicles and have no franchised motor vehicle dealers in New York\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo increase the certificates of registration for entities that manufac-\nture or assemble zero emissions vehicles to open sales locations in New\nYork State\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Removes the limit on the number of certificate of registra-\ntions the Commissioner of Motor Vehicles may issue and renew to manufac-\nturers that manufacture or assemble exclusively zero emission vehicles\n(ZEVs) and adds the requirement that an eligible manufacturer must not\nhave a franchised motor vehicle dealer of the same line-make in the\nState.\n\nSection 2: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, New York law only allows five electric vehicle (EV) direct\nsales locations throughout the State. Due to overwhelming demand, these\nstores are all located downstate, leaving upstate residents without\nconvenient and accessible locations to purchase zero-emission vehicles\n(ZEVs), and preventing any additional electric vehicle companies from\nopening stores in the State.\n\nThe Climate Leadership and Community Protection Act (CLCPA) requires the\nState to achieve a carbon-free electricity system by 2040, and codifies\nthe goals of a 40 percent reduction in greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions\nfrom 1990 levels by 2030, and 85 percent by 2050.  Additionally, the\nGovernor has set the goal of having 850,000 EVs on the road by 2025, and\ntwo million EVs by 2030 through the 2014 Multi-State ZEV Memorandum of\nUnderstanding (MOU). As of September 2020, New York had only achieved 3\npercent of the 2025 goal. New York cannot meet these goals and targets\nwithout increased adoption of electric vehicles. Allowing more direct\nsales locations from multiple EV manufacturers will increase near-term\neconomic investment and employment opportunities in retail and service\nlocations and make it easier for New Yorkers across the state to\npurchase electric vehicles.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
